Algorithm:

Step 1: Initialize two variables low = 0 and high = x.
Step 2: Start a Binary Search loop while low <= high.
Step 3: Find the middle value mid = (low + high) / 2.
Step 4: Calculate the square of mid using a long variable to avoid overflow.
Step 5: If the square is equal to x, return mid as the square root.
Step 6: If the square is greater than x, move the search to the left by updating high = mid - 1.
Step 7: If the square is smaller than x, move the search to the right by updating low = mid + 1.
Step 8: Continue this process until the search space becomes invalid (low > high).
Step 9: Return high, because it stores the largest number whose square is less than or equal to x.

Time Complexity: O(log x)
Space Complexity: O(1)


-----> Code <-----

class Solution {
    public int mySqrt(int x) {
        int low=0;
        int high=x;
        while(low<=high){
            int mid=(high+low)/2;
            long sq=(long)mid*mid;
            if(sq==x){
                return mid;
            }else if(sq>x){
                high=mid-1;
            }else{
                low=mid+1;
            }
        }
        return high;
    }
}
